Some changes only make sense together. Recording a sale means creating the order, adding its lines and reducing the stock. If the power fails halfway, you would have an order without lines, or stock reduced for nothing. A <b>transaction</b> groups several statements so that either all of them take effect, or none of them does.

### BEGIN and COMMIT

~~~sql
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, order_date, status) VALUES (9, '2025-05-10', 'pending');
INSERT INTO order_items (order_id, product_id, qty) VALUES (16, 7, 2);
UPDATE products SET stock = stock - 2 WHERE id = 7;
COMMIT;

SELECT o.id, c.name, oi.qty, p.name AS product, p.stock
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c    ON c.id = o.customer_id
JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.id
JOIN products p     ON p.id = oi.product_id
WHERE o.id = 16;
~~~

- BEGIN;: start a transaction. Changes made from now on are provisional.
- The INSERTs and the UPDATE: the three parts of the sale. The new order gets id 16, the next free number.
- COMMIT;: make every change since BEGIN permanent, all at once.

The SELECT shows order 16 for Ines Moreau: 2 Desk Lamps, with the lamp's stock now 58 instead of 60. Outside of BEGIN, each statement is its own tiny transaction, committed as soon as it finishes. This is called <b>autocommit</b>.

### ROLLBACK: undo everything since BEGIN

~~~sql
BEGIN;
DELETE FROM orders;
SELECT COUNT(*) AS during_transaction FROM orders;
ROLLBACK;

SELECT COUNT(*) AS after_rollback FROM orders;
~~~

- BEGIN;: start a transaction.
- DELETE FROM orders;: remove every order. Inside the transaction, the change is visible.
- <code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS during_transaction</code>: shows 0.
- ROLLBACK;: cancel every change since BEGIN, as if it never happened.
- <code>SELECT COUNT(*) AS after_rollback</code>: back to 15.

The page shows two result tables: 0 during the transaction, 15 after the rollback. In real code, you roll back when a step fails or when a check shows something is wrong.

### The classic example: a transfer

~~~sql
CREATE TABLE accounts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, owner TEXT, balance REAL);
INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1, 'Shop', 1000), (2, 'Supplier', 200);

BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 300 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 300 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;

SELECT * FROM accounts;
~~~

- The first two statements set up a small accounts table.
- Between BEGIN and COMMIT: debit one account, credit the other. Money must never leave one account without arriving in the other, so both updates belong in one transaction.

The SELECT shows Shop at 700 and Supplier at 500. The total, 1200, is unchanged, which is the point.

### Several transactions in one script

A script can hold several transactions, one after the other. Each is independent: a rollback in the first does not affect the second.

~~~sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled';
ROLLBACK;

BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET status = 'delivered' WHERE status = 'shipped';
COMMIT;

SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS n FROM orders GROUP BY status;
~~~

- First transaction: cancels every order by mistake, then ROLLBACK throws that away.
- Second transaction: marks the 2 shipped orders as delivered, then COMMIT keeps it.

The result has 3 rows: cancelled 2, delivered 12, pending 1. The mass cancellation left no trace; the second change stuck.

### Rules to remember

- A transaction must be closed with COMMIT or ROLLBACK before you start the next one. A BEGIN inside an open transaction fails with "cannot start a transaction within a transaction".
- Other users of the database do not see your changes until you COMMIT.
- If an error happens in the middle, the statements before it are still pending: decide to ROLLBACK (usually) or fix and COMMIT.

### Common mistakes

- Forgetting COMMIT, so the changes stay pending and are lost when the connection closes.
- Forgetting BEGIN, so each statement is committed on its own and ROLLBACK has nothing to undo.
- Expecting ROLLBACK to undo something that was already committed.

### Recap

- <code>BEGIN;</code> starts a transaction; <code>COMMIT;</code> makes its changes permanent; <code>ROLLBACK;</code> cancels them.
- All statements in a transaction succeed together or are undone together.
- Without BEGIN, each statement commits on its own (autocommit).
- Each transaction ends before the next one begins.
